\chi \sim \frac{c}{R}

﻿

for some relevant local curvature radius or radial scale R. Or alternatively:

﻿

\dot R_\tau-\dot R_s=c

﻿

which says the difference in radial growth speeds between the “time” direction and the ordinary spatial directions is c.

﻿

That is probably the cleanest version:

﻿

space = radial growth shared by three dimensions

time = the fourth radial growth component, offset by c

light = excitation riding the offset

matter = excitation coupled to / retarding the slower triad

mass = resistance produced by failing to align with the fastest growth mode

